<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Sepphoris</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Tzippori, Saffuriya, Diocaesarea</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Sepphoris is an ancient town in Lower <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/galilee">Galilee</a>, north-west of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nazareth">Nazareth</a>, with springs that made it an important stopping point. It was a leading Jewish and Roman city in antiquity, later the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/crusader-states">Crusader</a> village of La Sephorie and the Arab village of Saffuriya. In July 1187 the army of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-jerusalem">Kingdom of Jerusalem</a> gathered at its springs before marching east to its destruction by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saladin">Saladin</a> at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-hattin">Battle of Hattin</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/sepphoris.jpg" alt="Sepphoris" width="320" height="240" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Almog · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:%D7%AA%D7%9E%D7%95%D7%A0%D7%94_134.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
